#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550342 :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0c9b9b056aa08b09342da5daad12d23c40462d25e1a04088177b51de4f9b6af.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{20}{3}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{23}{3}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{20}{3}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{23}{3}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{400}{9}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{529}{9}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9{x}^{2}}{400}+\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{529} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551240 :question/problem "![](images/4e/4ee9e02184efd314371a99c6242540e5e2e6effc9e21d41a86648b433b8a6938.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semiellipse is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/1d/1d91a85ccbb948e070db738373ffc5032f4e91a0ba8e04cd14e79276b0e798f8.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e0bffd04fb7b765d3c654f62b1958ab55a42f0d26cbc9f2b2e31f7d8e6f775.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551246 :question/problem "![](images/9f/9f94742a7f37fd7f6a1c789401a2e323fec80aca584774453cba03cf69f28ade.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/f4/f4e8c62562ee1657e41bb36a21c901dc95935558641f724f934a4c58706f4a64.png)\n\n \n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/40/40b100dd4bc71b95f5ad99eb0cec8ed87133211bb371b22deccb851f79c695b3.png)\n\n \n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551293 :question/problem "What is the slope $m$ of the line $\\overset{↔}{AB}?$\n\n$m\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "As we found previously, the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,-3)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(1,3),$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, we compute the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-(-3)}{1-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{-3} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551486 :question/problem "$$\nf(z,y)=⟨?,\\,y,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(z,y)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $6{y}^{2}=x.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(z,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6{y}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $6{y}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551487 :question/problem "$$\nf(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,?⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,y)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=7{x}^{2}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,7{x}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $7{x}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551488 :question/problem "$$\nf(z,y)=⟨?,\\,y,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(z,y)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x=6{z}^{2}.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(z,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6{z}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $6{z}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551498 :question/problem "$$\nf(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,?⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,y)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $2{y}^{2}=z.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,2{y}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $2{y}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551503 :question/problem "$$\nf(z,y)=⟨?,\\,y,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(z,y)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $8{y}^{2}=x.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(z,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8{y}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $8{y}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551509 :question/problem "$$\nf(x,z)=⟨x,\\,?,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $12{z}^{2}=y.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,12{z}^{2},\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $12{z}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551520 :question/problem "$$\nf(z,y)=⟨?,\\,y,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(z,y)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x=7{z}^{2}.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(z,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7{z}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $7{z}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551528 :question/problem "![](images/51/512905447203d1d3a96781d20d586f9c4cf5cc9392fab50607b6fc77e9b00121.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,4),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,4)$ to the point $B(0,4).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=16,\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=(- 8 x)\\,i+(- 8 y)\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_{C_1}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r+\\int_{C_2}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path $C_1$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$r_1(t)=(t)\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j,$$\n\nwith $t$ running from $0$ to $2$. Along this path, $x=t$ and $y=t^{2}$. Therefore,\n\n$$F(r_1(t))=(- 8 t)\\,i+(- 8 t^{2})\\,j.$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$r_1'(t)=(1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j.$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$F(r_1(t))\\cdot r_1'(t)=(- 8 t)(1)+(- 8 t^{2})(2 t)=- 16 t^{3} - 8 t.$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\\int_{C_1}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_{0}^{2}(- 16 t^{3} - 8 t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t=\\left[- 4 t^{4} - 4 t^{2}\\right]_{0}^{2}=-80.$$\n\n$$\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=(-80)+(16)=-64.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551532 :question/problem "Evaluate $∇g(x,y,z)$ at the point $(1,3,1)$ if $g(x,y,z)=- \\frac{x y z}{3 x + y + z}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(- \\frac{x y z}{3 x + y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3 x + y + z)(- y z)-(- x y z)(3)}{(3 x + y + z)^2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{y z \\left(y + z\\right)}{\\left(3 x + y + z\\right)^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(- \\frac{x y z}{3 x + y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3 x + y + z)(- x z)-(- x y z)(1)}{(3 x + y + z)^2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{x z \\left(3 x + z\\right)}{\\left(3 x + y + z\\right)^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}\\left(- \\frac{x y z}{3 x + y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3 x + y + z)(- x y)-(- x y z)(1)}{(3 x + y + z)^2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{x y \\left(3 x + y\\right)}{\\left(3 x + y + z\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =(\\frac{3 x y z}{\\left(3 x + y + z\\right)^{2}} - \\frac{y z}{3 x + y + z})\\,\\mathbf i+(\\frac{x y z}{\\left(3 x + y + z\\right)^{2}} - \\frac{x z}{3 x + y + z})\\,\\mathbf j+(\\frac{x y z}{\\left(3 x + y + z\\right)^{2}} - \\frac{x y}{3 x + y + z})\\,\\mathbf k \\\\ ∇g(1,3,1) & =\\left(\\frac{(7)(-3)-(-3)(3)}{(7)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{(7)(-1)-(-3)(1)}{(7)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(\\frac{(7)(-3)-(-3)(1)}{(7)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{12}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{4}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{18}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{12}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{4}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{18}{49}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551543 :question/problem "Evaluate $∇g(x,y,z)$ at the point $(3,1,3)$ if $g(x,y,z)=- \\frac{2 x y z}{3 x - y + z}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(- \\frac{2 x y z}{3 x - y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3 x - y + z)(- 2 y z)-(- 2 x y z)(3)}{(3 x - y + z)^2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2 y z \\left(y - z\\right)}{\\left(3 x - y + z\\right)^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(- \\frac{2 x y z}{3 x - y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3 x - y + z)(- 2 x z)-(- 2 x y z)(-1)}{(3 x - y + z)^2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{2 x z \\left(3 x + z\\right)}{\\left(3 x - y + z\\right)^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}\\left(- \\frac{2 x y z}{3 x - y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3 x - y + z)(- 2 x y)-(- 2 x y z)(1)}{(3 x - y + z)^2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{2 x y \\left(3 x - y\\right)}{\\left(3 x - y + z\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =(\\frac{6 x y z}{\\left(3 x - y + z\\right)^{2}} - \\frac{2 y z}{3 x - y + z})\\,\\mathbf i+(- \\frac{2 x y z}{\\left(3 x - y + z\\right)^{2}} - \\frac{2 x z}{3 x - y + z})\\,\\mathbf j+(\\frac{2 x y z}{\\left(3 x - y + z\\right)^{2}} - \\frac{2 x y}{3 x - y + z})\\,\\mathbf k \\\\ ∇g(3,1,3) & =\\left(\\frac{(11)(-6)-(-18)(3)}{(11)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{(11)(-18)-(-18)(-1)}{(11)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(\\frac{(11)(-6)-(-18)(1)}{(11)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{12}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{216}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{48}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{12}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{216}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{48}{121}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551550 :question/problem "Evaluate $∇g(x,y,z)$ at the point $(-1,-3,1)$ if $g(x,y,z)=- \\frac{3 x y z}{- 2 x - 3 y + z}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(- \\frac{3 x y z}{- 2 x - 3 y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(- 2 x - 3 y + z)(- 3 y z)-(- 3 x y z)(-2)}{(- 2 x - 3 y + z)^2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3 y z \\left(3 y - z\\right)}{\\left(2 x + 3 y - z\\right)^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(- \\frac{3 x y z}{- 2 x - 3 y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(- 2 x - 3 y + z)(- 3 x z)-(- 3 x y z)(-3)}{(- 2 x - 3 y + z)^2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3 x z \\left(2 x - z\\right)}{\\left(2 x + 3 y - z\\right)^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}\\left(- \\frac{3 x y z}{- 2 x - 3 y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(- 2 x - 3 y + z)(- 3 x y)-(- 3 x y z)(1)}{(- 2 x - 3 y + z)^2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3 x y \\left(2 x + 3 y\\right)}{\\left(2 x + 3 y - z\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =(- \\frac{6 x y z}{\\left(- 2 x - 3 y + z\\right)^{2}} - \\frac{3 y z}{- 2 x - 3 y + z})\\,\\mathbf i+(- \\frac{9 x y z}{\\left(- 2 x - 3 y + z\\right)^{2}} - \\frac{3 x z}{- 2 x - 3 y + z})\\,\\mathbf j+(\\frac{3 x y z}{\\left(- 2 x - 3 y + z\\right)^{2}} - \\frac{3 x y}{- 2 x - 3 y + z})\\,\\mathbf k \\\\ ∇g(-1,-3,1) & =\\left(\\frac{(12)(9)-(-9)(-2)}{(12)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{(12)(3)-(-9)(-3)}{(12)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(\\frac{(12)(-9)-(-9)(1)}{(12)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{11}{16}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{11}{16}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551551 :question/problem "Find $∇g(x,y,z)$ given that $g(x,y,z)=2 x^{3} + 2 y^{3} + 2 z^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x^{3} + 2 y^{3} + 2 z^{2}) \\\\  & =(6 x^{2})+(0)+(0) \\\\  & =6 x^{2} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x^{3} + 2 y^{3} + 2 z^{2}) \\\\  & =(0)+(6 y^{2})+(0) \\\\  & =6 y^{2} \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2 x^{3} + 2 y^{3} + 2 z^{2}) \\\\  & =(0)+(0)+(4 z) \\\\  & =4 z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =(6 x^{2})\\,\\mathbf i+(6 y^{2})\\,\\mathbf j+(4 z)\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551559 :question/problem "Find $∇g(x,y,z)$ given that $g(x,y,z)=3 x^{3} - 3 y^{2} + z^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 x^{3} - 3 y^{2} + z^{3}) \\\\  & =(9 x^{2})+(0)+(0) \\\\  & =9 x^{2} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3 x^{3} - 3 y^{2} + z^{3}) \\\\  & =(0)+(- 6 y)+(0) \\\\  & =- 6 y \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3 x^{3} - 3 y^{2} + z^{3}) \\\\  & =(0)+(0)+(3 z^{2}) \\\\  & =3 z^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =(9 x^{2})\\,\\mathbf i+(- 6 y)\\,\\mathbf j+(3 z^{2})\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551560 :question/problem "Evaluate $∇g(x,y,z)$ at the point $(-2,-3,-3)$ if $g(x,y,z)=\\frac{x y z}{2 x + 3 y + z}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{x y z}{2 x + 3 y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2 x + 3 y + z)(y z)-(x y z)(2)}{(2 x + 3 y + z)^2} \\\\  & =\\frac{y z \\left(3 y + z\\right)}{\\left(2 x + 3 y + z\\right)^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x y z}{2 x + 3 y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2 x + 3 y + z)(x z)-(x y z)(3)}{(2 x + 3 y + z)^2} \\\\  & =\\frac{x z \\left(2 x + z\\right)}{\\left(2 x + 3 y + z\\right)^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}\\left(\\frac{x y z}{2 x + 3 y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2 x + 3 y + z)(x y)-(x y z)(1)}{(2 x + 3 y + z)^2} \\\\  & =\\frac{x y \\left(2 x + 3 y\\right)}{\\left(2 x + 3 y + z\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =(- \\frac{2 x y z}{\\left(2 x + 3 y + z\\right)^{2}} + \\frac{y z}{2 x + 3 y + z})\\,\\mathbf i+(- \\frac{3 x y z}{\\left(2 x + 3 y + z\\right)^{2}} + \\frac{x z}{2 x + 3 y + z})\\,\\mathbf j+(- \\frac{x y z}{\\left(2 x + 3 y + z\\right)^{2}} + \\frac{x y}{2 x + 3 y + z})\\,\\mathbf k \\\\ ∇g(-2,-3,-3) & =\\left(\\frac{(-16)(9)-(-18)(2)}{(-16)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{(-16)(6)-(-18)(3)}{(-16)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(\\frac{(-16)(6)-(-18)(1)}{(-16)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{27}{64}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{21}{128}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{39}{128}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{27}{64}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{21}{128}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(- \\frac{39}{128}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551568 :question/problem "Evaluate $∇g(x,y,z)$ at the point $(3,2,1)$ if $g(x,y,z)=\\frac{x y z}{3 x - 2 y + z}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{x y z}{3 x - 2 y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3 x - 2 y + z)(y z)-(x y z)(3)}{(3 x - 2 y + z)^2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{y z \\left(2 y - z\\right)}{\\left(3 x - 2 y + z\\right)^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x y z}{3 x - 2 y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3 x - 2 y + z)(x z)-(x y z)(-2)}{(3 x - 2 y + z)^2} \\\\  & =\\frac{x z \\left(3 x + z\\right)}{\\left(3 x - 2 y + z\\right)^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}\\left(\\frac{x y z}{3 x - 2 y + z}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3 x - 2 y + z)(x y)-(x y z)(1)}{(3 x - 2 y + z)^2} \\\\  & =\\frac{x y \\left(3 x - 2 y\\right)}{\\left(3 x - 2 y + z\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =(- \\frac{3 x y z}{\\left(3 x - 2 y + z\\right)^{2}} + \\frac{y z}{3 x - 2 y + z})\\,\\mathbf i+(\\frac{2 x y z}{\\left(3 x - 2 y + z\\right)^{2}} + \\frac{x z}{3 x - 2 y + z})\\,\\mathbf j+(- \\frac{x y z}{\\left(3 x - 2 y + z\\right)^{2}} + \\frac{x y}{3 x - 2 y + z})\\,\\mathbf k \\\\ ∇g(3,2,1) & =\\left(\\frac{(6)(2)-(6)(3)}{(6)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{(6)(3)-(6)(-2)}{(6)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(\\frac{(6)(6)-(6)(1)}{(6)^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{1}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{1}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551571 :question/problem "Find $∇g(x,y,z)$ given that $g(x,y,z)=- 2 x^{2} - y^{3} + 3 z^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 2 x^{2} - y^{3} + 3 z^{3}) \\\\  & =(- 4 x)+(0)+(0) \\\\  & =- 4 x \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 2 x^{2} - y^{3} + 3 z^{3}) \\\\  & =(0)+(- 3 y^{2})+(0) \\\\  & =- 3 y^{2} \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(- 2 x^{2} - y^{3} + 3 z^{3}) \\\\  & =(0)+(0)+(9 z^{2}) \\\\  & =9 z^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =(- 4 x)\\,\\mathbf i+(- 3 y^{2})\\,\\mathbf j+(9 z^{2})\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551586 :question/problem "Find $∇g(x,y,z)$ given that $g(x,y,z)=2 x^{3} - 2 y^{3} + 5 z^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x^{3} - 2 y^{3} + 5 z^{3}) \\\\  & =(6 x^{2})+(0)+(0) \\\\  & =6 x^{2} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x^{3} - 2 y^{3} + 5 z^{3}) \\\\  & =(0)+(- 6 y^{2})+(0) \\\\  & =- 6 y^{2} \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2 x^{3} - 2 y^{3} + 5 z^{3}) \\\\  & =(0)+(0)+(15 z^{2}) \\\\  & =15 z^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =(6 x^{2})\\,\\mathbf i+(- 6 y^{2})\\,\\mathbf j+(15 z^{2})\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551595 :question/problem "Find $∇g(x,y,z)$ given that $g(x,y,z)=x^{3} + 2 y^{2} + 2 z^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{3} + 2 y^{2} + 2 z^{3}) \\\\  & =(3 x^{2})+(0)+(0) \\\\  & =3 x^{2} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{3} + 2 y^{2} + 2 z^{3}) \\\\  & =(0)+(4 y)+(0) \\\\  & =4 y \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x^{3} + 2 y^{2} + 2 z^{3}) \\\\  & =(0)+(0)+(6 z^{2}) \\\\  & =6 z^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\  & =(3 x^{2})\\,\\mathbf i+(4 y)\\,\\mathbf j+(6 z^{2})\\,\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552430 :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={y}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2+x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{3}=8{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2+x=2+4\\operatorname{cos}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,du=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(8{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}t)-(2+4\\operatorname{cos}t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t\\operatorname{cos}t+8\\operatorname{sin}t+16\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t\\operatorname{cos}t+16\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{0}16{u}^{3}+16u\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552465 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=3xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ If the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $\\mathbf{n}=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/38/386f010932b5846e7f47a605a94a5b5d580bd35dbe37512b1583d15d29f3dc70.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(3xy\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (xi+y\\,j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}3{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}2{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $2{x}^{2}y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552470 :question/problem "![](images/0d/0dd48f19e9940f1684c0cbb723e772384dda3d7429fc89c2c9dc528f7ebfe42c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ shown above. If the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across the side ${C}_{4}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{4}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $y$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=-j.$\n\n![](images/c5/c591fedf1b1c8ea705f0c13fbfd96bc7f1f6701841e3eff03bd0578aa2f8ab06.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}(xy\\,i-{x}^{2}y\\,j)\\cdot (-j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552492 :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{2}=4{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-xy=-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt=du,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}t)-(-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\operatorname{cos}t-2{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6\\operatorname{cos}t{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0}6{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552504 :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+8\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }32{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }32\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+16t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(0+32\\pi -1)-(0-1) \\\\  & =32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552629 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\left(y \\left(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left(x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\left(e^{4 t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\left(y \\left(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left(x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=e^{4 t}$ and $y=\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\left(y \\left(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left(x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,j\\Big|_{x=e^{4 t},\\,y=\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}} \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(4 t + 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(4 t e^{4 t}\\right)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(e^{4 t}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(4 e^{4 t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\left(4 t + 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(4 t e^{4 t}\\right)\\,j\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(4 e^{4 t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(4 t + 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(4 e^{4 t}\\right)+\\left(4 t e^{4 t}\\right)\\cdot \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(16 t e^{4 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 4 e^{4 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(12 t e^{4 t} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =4 \\left(4 t \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 t \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{4 t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi}{6},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]\\Big|_{t=\\frac{\\pi}{6}} & =4 \\left(4 t \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 t \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{4 t}\\Big|_{t=\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{4 \\left(3 + 2 \\pi\\right) e^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}}{3}\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\left(3 + 2 \\pi\\right) e^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552637 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi}{8}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\left(2 x + 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 x + 3 y\\right)\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\left(2 x + 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 x + 3 y\\right)\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}$ and $y=\\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\left(2 x + 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 x + 3 y\\right)\\,j\\Big|_{x=\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)},\\,y=\\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}} \\\\  & =\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 \\sqrt{2} \\sin{\\left(2 t + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}\\right)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 \\sqrt{2} \\sin{\\left(2 t + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}\\right)\\,j\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(3 \\sqrt{2} \\sin{\\left(2 t + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(4 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 6 \\sqrt{2} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\sin{\\left(2 t + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =- \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 12 \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)} - 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi}{8},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]\\Big|_{t=\\frac{\\pi}{8}} & =- \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 12 \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)} - 6\\Big|_{t=\\frac{\\pi}{8}} \\\\  & =\\left(5\\right)+\\left(-6\\right) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552639 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi}{12}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\left(2 x + 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 x + 3 y\\right)\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\left(\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\left(2 x + 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 x + 3 y\\right)\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}$ and $y=\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\left(2 x + 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 x + 3 y\\right)\\,j\\Big|_{x=\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)},\\,y=\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}} \\\\  & =\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 \\sqrt{2} \\sin{\\left(3 t + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}\\right)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 \\sqrt{2} \\sin{\\left(3 t + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}\\right)\\,j\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(3 \\sqrt{2} \\sin{\\left(3 t + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(6 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 9 \\cos^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 9 \\sqrt{2} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} \\sin{\\left(3 t + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\left(- \\sin{\\left(6 t \\right)} + 6 \\cos{\\left(6 t \\right)}\\right)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi}{12},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]\\Big|_{t=\\frac{\\pi}{12}} & =\\frac{3 \\left(- \\sin{\\left(6 t \\right)} + 6 \\cos{\\left(6 t \\right)}\\right)}{2}\\Big|_{t=\\frac{\\pi}{12}} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{15}{2}\\right)+\\left(-9\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552653 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{5 \\pi}{4}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\left(2 x + 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 x + 3 y\\right)\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\left(2 x + 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 x + 3 y\\right)\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\left(2 x + 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 x + 3 y\\right)\\,j\\Big|_{x=\\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\,y=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}} \\\\  & =\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 \\sqrt{2} \\sin{\\left(t + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}\\right)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 \\sqrt{2} \\sin{\\left(t + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}\\right)\\,j\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,j\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(3 \\sqrt{2} \\sin{\\left(t + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\sqrt{2} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\sin{\\left(t + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{- \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 6 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{5 \\pi}{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]\\Big|_{t=\\frac{5 \\pi}{4}} & =\\frac{- \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 6 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}\\Big|_{t=\\frac{5 \\pi}{4}} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)+\\left(-3\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552655 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\left(2 y \\left(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\left(e^{3 t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\left(2 y \\left(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=e^{3 t}$ and $y=\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\left(2 y \\left(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,j\\Big|_{x=e^{3 t},\\,y=\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}} \\\\  & =\\left(2 \\left(3 t + 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(6 t e^{3 t}\\right)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(e^{3 t}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(3 e^{3 t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(2 \\left(3 t + 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(6 t e^{3 t}\\right)\\,j\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(3 e^{3 t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(2 \\left(3 t + 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(3 e^{3 t}\\right)+\\left(6 t e^{3 t}\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(18 t e^{3 t} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 6 e^{3 t} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(12 t e^{3 t} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =6 \\left(3 t \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 2 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi}{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]\\Big|_{t=\\frac{\\pi}{4}} & =6 \\left(3 t \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 2 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t}\\Big|_{t=\\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3 \\left(4 + 3 \\pi\\right) e^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}}}{2}\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\left(4 + 3 \\pi\\right) e^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552664 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\left(2 x + 2 y e^{x y}\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 x e^{x y} + 2 y\\right)\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\left(3 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(t\\right)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\left(2 x + 2 y e^{x y}\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 x e^{x y} + 2 y\\right)\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=3 t^{2}$ and $y=t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\left(2 x + 2 y e^{x y}\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 x e^{x y} + 2 y\\right)\\,j\\Big|_{x=3 t^{2},\\,y=t} \\\\  & =\\left(2 t \\left(3 t + e^{3 t^{3}}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 t \\left(3 t e^{3 t^{3}} + 1\\right)\\right)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(3 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(t\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(6 t\\right)\\,i+\\left(1\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(2 t \\left(3 t + e^{3 t^{3}}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 t \\left(3 t e^{3 t^{3}} + 1\\right)\\right)\\,j\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(6 t\\right)\\,i+\\left(1\\right)\\,j\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(2 t \\left(3 t + e^{3 t^{3}}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(6 t\\right)+\\left(2 t \\left(3 t e^{3 t^{3}} + 1\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(36 t^{3} + 12 t^{2} e^{3 t^{3}}\\right)+\\left(6 t^{2} e^{3 t^{3}} + 2 t\\right) \\\\  & =2 t \\left(18 t^{2} + 9 t e^{3 t^{3}} + 1\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]\\Big|_{t=1} & =2 t \\left(18 t^{2} + 9 t e^{3 t^{3}} + 1\\right)\\Big|_{t=1} \\\\  & =\\left(12 \\left(3 + e^{3}\\right)\\right)+\\left(2 \\left(1 + 3 e^{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =2 \\left(19 + 9 e^{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552665 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{5 \\pi}{12}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 x + 2 y\\right)\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\left(\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 x + 2 y\\right)\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}$ and $y=\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 x + 2 y\\right)\\,j\\Big|_{x=\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)},\\,y=\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}} \\\\  & =\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 \\sqrt{2} \\sin{\\left(3 t + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}\\right)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 \\sqrt{2} \\sin{\\left(3 t + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}\\right)\\,j\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(2 \\sqrt{2} \\sin{\\left(3 t + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(9 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 6 \\sqrt{2} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} \\sin{\\left(3 t + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\left(\\sin{\\left(6 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(6 t \\right)}\\right)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{5 \\pi}{12},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]\\Big|_{t=\\frac{5 \\pi}{12}} & =\\frac{3 \\left(\\sin{\\left(6 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(6 t \\right)}\\right)}{2}\\Big|_{t=\\frac{5 \\pi}{12}} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{15}{2}\\right)+\\left(-6\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552669 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\left(2 y \\left(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\left(e^{4 t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\left(2 y \\left(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=e^{4 t}$ and $y=\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\left(2 y \\left(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,j\\Big|_{x=e^{4 t},\\,y=\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}} \\\\  & =\\left(2 \\left(4 t + 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(8 t e^{4 t}\\right)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(e^{4 t}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(4 e^{4 t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(2 \\left(4 t + 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(8 t e^{4 t}\\right)\\,j\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(4 e^{4 t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\,j\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(2 \\left(4 t + 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(4 e^{4 t}\\right)+\\left(8 t e^{4 t}\\right)\\cdot \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(32 t e^{4 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 8 e^{4 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(24 t e^{4 t} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =8 \\left(4 t \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 t \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{4 t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi}{6},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]\\Big|_{t=\\frac{\\pi}{6}} & =8 \\left(4 t \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 t \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{4 t}\\Big|_{t=\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{8 \\left(3 + 2 \\pi\\right) e^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}}{3}\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{8 \\left(3 + 2 \\pi\\right) e^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552684 :question/problem "If $f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ - e^{u} \\\\ 2 u^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=3 t^{3} - 3 t + 1$, then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$ Write the polynomial component in expanded form." :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)=f′(u(t))u′(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(- e^{u}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(2 u^{2}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ - e^{u} \\\\ 4 u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ u^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(3 t^{3} - 3 t + 1\\right)=9 t^{2} - 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & =f′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 \\cos{\\left(u(t) \\right)} \\\\ - e^{u(t)} \\\\ 4 u(t)\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(u^{′}(t)\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 \\cos{\\left(3 t^{3} - 3 t + 1 \\right)} \\\\ - e^{3 t^{3} - 3 t + 1} \\\\ 12 t^{3} - 12 t + 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(9 t^{2} - 3\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(6 - 18 t^{2}\\right) \\cos{\\left(3 t^{3} - 3 t + 1 \\right)} \\\\ \\left(3 - 9 t^{2}\\right) e^{3 t^{3} - 3 t + 1} \\\\ 108 t^{5} - 144 t^{3} + 36 t^{2} + 36 t - 12\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552696 :question/problem "If $f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ 2 e^{u} \\\\ - 3 u^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=- 2 t^{3} + 3 t + 2$, then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$ Write the polynomial component in expanded form." :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)=f′(u(t))u′(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(2 e^{u}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(- 3 u^{2}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ 2 e^{u} \\\\ - 6 u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ u^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(- 2 t^{3} + 3 t + 2\\right)=3 - 6 t^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & =f′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 \\cos{\\left(u(t) \\right)} \\\\ 2 e^{u(t)} \\\\ - 6 u(t)\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(u^{′}(t)\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 \\cos{\\left(- 2 t^{3} + 3 t + 2 \\right)} \\\\ 2 e^{- 2 t^{3} + 3 t + 2} \\\\ 12 t^{3} - 18 t - 12\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(3 - 6 t^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(12 t^{2} - 6\\right) \\cos{\\left(- 2 t^{3} + 3 t + 2 \\right)} \\\\ \\left(6 - 12 t^{2}\\right) e^{- 2 t^{3} + 3 t + 2} \\\\ - 72 t^{5} + 144 t^{3} + 72 t^{2} - 54 t - 36\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552698 :question/problem "If $f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ 3 e^{u} \\\\ u^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=- t^{3} + t + 1$, then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$ Write the polynomial component in expanded form." :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)=f′(u(t))u′(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(- \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(3 e^{u}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(u^{2}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\cos{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ 3 e^{u} \\\\ 2 u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ u^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(- t^{3} + t + 1\\right)=1 - 3 t^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & =f′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}- \\cos{\\left(u(t) \\right)} \\\\ 3 e^{u(t)} \\\\ 2 u(t)\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(u^{′}(t)\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}- \\cos{\\left(- t^{3} + t + 1 \\right)} \\\\ 3 e^{- t^{3} + t + 1} \\\\ - 2 t^{3} + 2 t + 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(1 - 3 t^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(3 t^{2} - 1\\right) \\cos{\\left(- t^{3} + t + 1 \\right)} \\\\ \\left(3 - 9 t^{2}\\right) e^{- t^{3} + t + 1} \\\\ 6 t^{5} - 8 t^{3} - 6 t^{2} + 2 t + 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552702 :question/problem "If $f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ 2 e^{u} \\\\ - 2 u^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=- 2 t^{2} + t + 2$, then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)=f′(u(t))u′(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(2 e^{u}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(- 2 u^{2}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ 2 e^{u} \\\\ - 4 u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ u^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(- 2 t^{2} + t + 2\\right)=1 - 4 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & =f′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cos{\\left(u(t) \\right)} \\\\ 2 e^{u(t)} \\\\ - 4 u(t)\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(u^{′}(t)\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cos{\\left(- 2 t^{2} + t + 2 \\right)} \\\\ 2 e^{- 2 t^{2} + t + 2} \\\\ 8 t^{2} - 4 t - 8\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(1 - 4 t\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(2 - 8 t\\right) \\cos{\\left(- 2 t^{2} + t + 2 \\right)} \\\\ \\left(2 - 8 t\\right) e^{- 2 t^{2} + t + 2} \\\\ 4 \\left(4 t - 1\\right) \\left(- 2 t^{2} + t + 2\\right)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552719 :question/problem "If $f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ - 2 e^{u} \\\\ - u^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=- 2 t^{3} - 3 t + 2$, then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$ Write the polynomial component in expanded form." :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)=f′(u(t))u′(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(- \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(- 2 e^{u}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(- u^{2}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\cos{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ - 2 e^{u} \\\\ - 2 u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ u^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(- 2 t^{3} - 3 t + 2\\right)=- 6 t^{2} - 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & =f′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}- \\cos{\\left(u(t) \\right)} \\\\ - 2 e^{u(t)} \\\\ - 2 u(t)\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(u^{′}(t)\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}- \\cos{\\left(2 t^{3} + 3 t - 2 \\right)} \\\\ - 2 e^{- 2 t^{3} - 3 t + 2} \\\\ 4 t^{3} + 6 t - 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(- 6 t^{2} - 3\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(6 t^{2} + 3\\right) \\cos{\\left(2 t^{3} + 3 t - 2 \\right)} \\\\ \\left(12 t^{2} + 6\\right) e^{- 2 t^{3} - 3 t + 2} \\\\ - 24 t^{5} - 48 t^{3} + 24 t^{2} - 18 t + 12\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
